{"type":"text","timestamp":1795789885814,"sessionID":"ses_de295c1feffeBBdrJFVBgaasT6","part":{"id":"prt_21d6b0b8b00153x2n2r8Te7I4m","sessionID":"ses_de295c1feffeBBdrJFVBgaasT6","messageID":"msg_21d6afe9f001iPWxhbOX4jW2u1","type":"text","text":"Done. Here is the proposal and what I implemented.\n\n## Proposal\n\nIntroduce a one-method interface, `IntOperation`, to represent a binary integer operation. This separates the *operation* from the code that *invokes* it, so new integer operations can be added without touching the caller. `add` remains as a thin convenience wrapper, keeping the existing API and test intact.\n\n## Implementation\n\n`main.go`:\n- Added `IntOperation` with a single `Apply(a, b int) int` method (narrow by design).\n- Added `addOperation`, the concrete addition implementation.\n- Added `apply(op, a, b)`, which invokes any `IntOperation`.\n- Rewrote `add` to delegate through `apply(addOperation{}, a, b)`.\n\n`main_test.go`:\n- Kept `TestAdd` unchanged.\n- Added `TestApplyAcceptsCustomIntOperation` with a caller-supplied `multiplyOperation`, proving `apply` is decoupled from addition.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `go build ./...` passes\n- `go test ./...` passes (both tests)\n- `go vet ./...` passes\n- `gofmt -l` reports no formatting issues\n\nNo new dependencies were added, and `add`'s signature is unchanged, so the module still builds and existing behavior is preserved.","time":{"start":1795789884299,"end":1795789885814}}}
